q('Burning and numbness start in both feet and ascend gradually before the hands become involved. Which distribution is most likely?', 'Length-dependent distal symmetric polyneuropathy',[
 ('A single cervical root lesion','One root does not create symmetric stocking-first loss.'),('A unilateral median neuropathy','This affects a specific hand territory.'),('A sensory ganglionopathy in every case','Ganglionopathy is often non-length-dependent and asymmetric.')],
 'The longest axons are affected first', '**A stocking-then-glove pattern supports length-dependent neuropathy.**', ['Ankle reflexes and distal vibration may decline.','Diabetes, toxic, metabolic and hereditary causes are common categories.'], '**Characterize tempo and fiber type.**', ['Rapid, asymmetric or motor-predominant disease warrants broader urgent assessment.','Do not assume diabetes explains every atypical pattern.'], [['Length dependence','Feet before hands'],['Symmetry','Supports generalized process'],['Atypical pattern','Reconsider cause']], [EV,PN], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which tests are especially high-yield when evaluating otherwise unexplained distal symmetric polyneuropathy?', 'Glucose assessment, B12 with appropriate metabolite testing and serum protein testing with immunofixation',[
 ('Routine broad neural antibody panels in every patient','Indiscriminate testing increases incidental and misleading results.'),('Brain amyloid PET as the initial neuropathy test','This does not investigate the peripheral syndrome.'),('Nerve biopsy before history and basic laboratory assessment','Biopsy is reserved for selected diagnostic questions.')],
 'Start with common and actionable causes', '**Glucose, B12 and monoclonal-protein assessment are high-yield components.**', ['Blood counts, renal, liver, thyroid and other tests depend on context.','Medication, alcohol and occupational exposure histories remain essential.'], '**Expand testing according to the phenotype.**', ['Use infectious, autoimmune or genetic tests when indicated.','Avoid a fixed exhaustive panel without a clinical question.'], [['Metabolic','Glucose and systemic assessment'],['Nutritional','B12 and selected metabolites'],['Paraprotein','Protein testing/immunofixation']], [EV])
q('A patient has painful burning feet and reduced temperature sensation but preserved vibration, strength and routine nerve-conduction studies. Which process is most likely?', 'Small-fiber neuropathy',[
 ('Normal NCS excludes all peripheral neuropathy','Routine NCS mainly assess large myelinated fibers.'),('A pure large-fiber sensory neuropathy','Large-fiber dysfunction more often impairs vibration/proprioception.'),('A motor-neuron disorder as the best explanation','The painful sensory phenotype does not fit a pure motor syndrome.')],
 'Routine electrophysiology does not sample every fiber class', '**Small-fiber disease can affect pain, temperature and autonomic function with normal routine NCS.**', ['Skin biopsy for intraepidermal nerve-fiber density can support diagnosis.','Autonomic testing may be useful in the appropriate setting.'], '**Investigate the cause as well as confirming the pattern.**', ['Consider glucose dysregulation, immune and toxic causes.','Clinical correlation prevents overinterpretation of isolated test results.'], [['Small fibers','Pain/temperature/autonomic'],['Large fibers','Vibration/proprioception'],['Routine NCS','May be normal in small-fiber disease']], [EV], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient becomes markedly unsteady with eyes closed and has absent toe-position sense and reduced vibration. Which sensory system is most implicated?', 'Large-fiber proprioceptive pathways',[
 ('Isolated small-fiber pain pathways','These do not primarily mediate joint position.'),('Pure motor endplate transmission','This does not cause proprioceptive loss.'),('A positive Romberg sign proves cerebellar degeneration','Visual dependence with position-sense loss supports sensory ataxia.')],
 'Vision can compensate for impaired proprioception', '**Large-fiber dysfunction can cause sensory ataxia and a positive Romberg sign.**', ['Posterior-column disease is another anatomical possibility.','Assess reflexes and distribution to distinguish peripheral from cord disease.'], '**Localize before assigning an etiology.**', ['Consider nutritional, immune, hereditary and toxic causes.','Use appropriate nerve studies and imaging when needed.'], [['Position/vibration','Large-fiber/posterior-column pathways'],['Eyes closed','Removes visual compensation'],['Localization','Peripheral or central assessment']], [EV,PN], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Abrupt painful foot drop is followed weeks later by weakness and numbness in a different noncontiguous nerve territory. Which pattern is most concerning?', 'Mononeuritis multiplex, including a possible vasculitic neuropathy',[
 ('Ordinary slowly progressive symmetric diabetic polyneuropathy alone','The painful stepwise asymmetric pattern is atypical.'),('A single entrapment neuropathy','Separate nerve territories require a multifocal explanation.'),('Pure myasthenia gravis','MG does not produce painful focal sensory loss.')],
 'Patchy ischemic nerve injury can evolve stepwise', '**Painful asymmetric axonal deficits suggest a vasculitic or other multifocal nerve process.**', ['Systemic vasculitis, infection, infiltration and other causes need assessment.','Later confluent injury can appear more symmetric.'], '**Arrange expedited investigation.**', ['Targeted nerve and sometimes muscle biopsy may be useful.','Treat the defined cause rather than assuming a benign entrapment.'], [['Distribution','Noncontiguous nerves'],['Tempo','Often stepwise'],['Concern','Vasculitic/infiltrative process']], [VA,PN], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which feature favors sensory neuronopathy over ordinary length-dependent polyneuropathy?', 'Non-length-dependent sensory loss with marked ataxia and disproportionate upper-limb involvement',[
 ('Strictly symmetric toes-first loss alone','This is a typical length-dependent pattern.'),('Pure fatigable ptosis with normal sensation','This suggests a junctional syndrome.'),('Isolated distal motor weakness without sensory findings','This is not the characteristic sensory-neuron pattern.')],
 'The dorsal-root ganglion can be the primary site', '**Sensory neuronopathy can produce widespread asymmetric sensory loss and ataxia.**', ['Sensory responses may be reduced in a non-length-dependent pattern.','Sjögren disease, paraneoplastic and toxic causes are important considerations.'], '**Investigate the pattern rather than using a generic neuropathy label.**', ['Review cancer and autoimmune clues.','A high-dose pyridoxine supplement history may be relevant.'], [['Ganglionopathy','Non-length-dependent'],['Distal axonopathy','Length-dependent'],['Clue','Severe sensory ataxia']], [EV])
q('A patient taking high-dose vitamin B6 supplements develops progressive sensory ataxia. Which action is most appropriate?', 'Review and stop excessive pyridoxine exposure while investigating the sensory neuropathy',[
 ('Increase B6 because all vitamins are harmless','Excess pyridoxine can be neurotoxic.'),('Diagnose B6 deficiency solely from supplement use','The dose and phenotype suggest potential toxicity.'),('Treat with IVIG without exposure review','A toxic cause must be considered before immunotherapy.')],
 'A supplement can be a neurological exposure', '**Excess pyridoxine can cause sensory neuropathy or neuronopathy.**', ['Ask about multiple overlapping supplements.','Dose, duration and other causes require assessment.'], '**Manage the exposure and monitor recovery.**', ['Recovery may be incomplete or slow.','Do not assume that vitamin labeling guarantees safety.'], [['Exposure','Excess pyridoxine'],['Pattern','Sensory-predominant injury'],['Action','Withdraw excess and assess']], [EV], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Longstanding diabetes is present, but neuropathy becomes rapidly progressive, asymmetric and proximally weak. What is the best diagnostic principle?', 'Investigate an additional or alternative neuropathic disorder rather than attributing every deficit to distal diabetic neuropathy',[
 ('Diabetes excludes all inflammatory neuropathies','The diseases can coexist.'),('Any diabetic patient automatically meets CIDP criteria','CIDP requires its own clinical and physiological evidence.'),('Atypical distribution eliminates the need for testing','It increases the need for focused evaluation.')],
 'A common diagnosis must still fit the pattern', '**Ordinary diabetic DSP is generally length-dependent and slowly progressive.**', ['Painful radiculoplexus neuropathy and entrapments are additional diabetic associations.','Inflammatory, vasculitic, neoplastic and other causes remain possible.'], '**Use tempo, distribution and electrophysiology.**', ['Avoid both reflexive diabetic attribution and reflexive CIDP labeling.','Assess objective findings and treat the defined process.'], [['Typical DSP','Slow, distal, symmetric'],['Atypical course','Broader evaluation'],['CIDP','Independent criteria needed']], [EV], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Severe unilateral thigh pain is followed by weight loss and proximal leg weakness in a patient with diabetes. Which diagnosis is most characteristic?', 'Diabetic lumbosacral radiculoplexus neuropathy',[
 ('Ordinary painless stocking neuropathy','The painful proximal asymmetric presentation differs.'),('A pure common fibular entrapment','This would not explain the proximal thigh pattern.'),('Myasthenic crisis','The focal painful presentation is not typical of junctional failure.')],
 'Diabetic neuropathy has more than one phenotype', '**A painful asymmetric proximal syndrome can involve roots, plexus and peripheral nerves.**', ['Weakness may spread and recovery can be prolonged.','Weight loss is a frequent clue but also prompts evaluation for malignancy.'], '**Exclude structural and other plexus disease.**', ['Use examination, electrophysiology and imaging as appropriate.','Provide pain control, nutrition and rehabilitation.'], [['Pain','Often severe and proximal'],['Weakness','Asymmetric proximal predominance'],['Differential','Structural/neoplastic plexopathy']], [EV,PN], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which treatment class is an evidence-based option for painful diabetic polyneuropathy?', 'An SNRI such as duloxetine, a gabapentinoid or another guideline-supported class chosen for the individual',[
 ('Routine long-term opioid therapy as preferred first-line care','AAN recommends against opioids for PDN.'),('High-dose corticosteroids for every diabetic sensory pain syndrome','This is not standard DSP pain treatment.'),('Antibiotics without an infectious indication','They do not treat ordinary neuropathic pain.')],
 'Treat pain while considering function and comorbidity', '**Several drug classes have broadly comparable average effects.**', ['Choice depends on sleep, mood, kidney function, falls and interactions.','No agent guarantees complete pain abolition.'], '**Measure meaningful benefit and tolerability.**', ['Consider a different effective class when the first is inadequate.','Continue foot protection and disease management.'], [['SNRI','Pain and selected mood benefit'],['Gabapentinoid','Dose/sedation/renal review'],['Opioid','Not recommended for PDN']], [PAIN])
q('Gabapentin provides little useful pain relief and causes sedation at a tolerated regimen. What is an appropriate next principle for painful diabetic neuropathy?', 'Consider a trial from a different effective medication class, guided by comorbidity and adverse effects',[
 ('Continue escalating despite harmful sedation indefinitely','Adverse effects can outweigh benefit.'),('Add an opioid automatically as the required next step','This conflicts with the AAN recommendation.'),('Conclude that the pain must be fabricated','Variable treatment response does not invalidate symptoms.')],
 'A class switch can be more useful than indefinite dose escalation', '**AAN recommends considering a different effective class after inadequate benefit or significant adverse effects.**', ['Review adherence, dose and diagnosis.','Mood and sleep influence the overall pain burden.'], '**Agree on functional goals.**', ['Use a safe taper or transition when required.','Avoid multiplying sedating medicines without reassessment.'], [['Poor efficacy','Reassess and consider class switch'],['Toxicity','Limits acceptable exposure'],['Target','Function as well as pain']], [PAIN], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which intervention prevents an important complication of insensate diabetic feet?', 'Regular foot inspection, appropriate footwear and prompt assessment of skin injury',[
 ('Walking barefoot to increase sensory stimulation','This increases unnoticed injury risk.'),('Waiting for pain before examining a wound','Loss of protective sensation can make serious lesions painless.'),('Pain medication alone as ulcer prevention','Analgesia does not replace protection and surveillance.')],
 'Reduced pain perception removes an injury warning', '**Neuropathy can conceal pressure injury, burns and infection.**', ['Inspect soles and between toes.','Assess vascular disease and deformity as additional risks.'], '**Make prevention practical.**', ['Arrange podiatric and footwear support when needed.','A warm swollen or injured foot deserves prompt review.'], [['Protective sensation','May be lost'],['Daily review','Detects unnoticed injury'],['Footwear','Reduces trauma/pressure']], [PN,EV])
q('Distal wasting, pes cavus and slowly progressive weakness begin in adolescence, with similar features in a parent. Which diagnosis is most likely?', 'Charcot–Marie–Tooth hereditary neuropathy',[
 ('An acute GBS attack','The lifelong familial course is inconsistent with an acute monophasic illness.'),('A single lumbar root lesion','This does not explain bilateral deformity and family history.'),('An isolated neuromuscular-junction disorder','Sensory/reflex changes and distal deformity support neuropathy.')],
 'A chronic inherited pattern deserves genetic consideration', '**CMT is a heterogeneous inherited peripheral neuropathy group.**', ['Distal weakness, reduced reflexes and foot deformity are common.','Family history may be absent because of de novo disease or inheritance patterns.'], '**Use physiology and phenotype to guide testing.**', ['Genetic counseling explains inheritance and uncertainty.','Orthoses, foot care and rehabilitation are central.'], [['Course','Longstanding progressive'],['Distribution','Distal neuropathy'],['Clue','Pes cavus/family history']], [CMT], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which common copy-number change causes CMT1A?', 'Duplication involving PMP22',[
 ('Deletion involving PMP22 as the usual CMT1A mechanism','Deletion more typically causes HNPP.'),('HTT CAG expansion','This causes Huntington disease.'),('DMPK CTG expansion','This causes myotonic dystrophy type 1.')],
 'Gene dosage produces related but distinct phenotypes', '**PMP22 duplication is a common cause of demyelinating CMT.**', ['Deletion/duplication testing is important in the genetic approach.','Other genes produce many additional CMT forms.'], '**Do not stop at a phenotype label when testing can clarify counseling.**', ['Interpret variants with a suitable service.','A negative PMP22 test does not exclude every hereditary neuropathy.'], [['PMP22 duplication','CMT1A'],['PMP22 deletion','Often HNPP'],['Other genes','Many CMT subtypes']], [CMT,HN])
q('Recurrent transient ulnar palsy after leaning on an elbow and fibular palsy after crossing the legs occur in several relatives. Which disorder is most likely?', 'Hereditary neuropathy with liability to pressure palsies',[
 ('CMT1A from the typical PMP22 duplication as the best fit','Repeated pressure-triggered palsies particularly suggest HNPP.'),('A single acquired cervical radiculopathy','This does not explain lower-limb episodes and familial susceptibility.'),('A primary myopathy','Focal pressure-related sensory-motor palsies localize to nerves.')],
 'Compression susceptibility can be inherited', '**HNPP commonly results from a PMP22 deletion.**', ['Minor compression can cause focal deficits.','Recovery varies and residual deficits can accumulate.'], '**Reduce avoidable nerve pressure.**', ['Discuss posture, repetitive activities and surgical positioning.','Offer genetic confirmation and counseling when appropriate.'], [['Trigger','Minor pressure'],['Common gene change','PMP22 deletion'],['Prevention','Avoid sustained compression']], [HN], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Progressive painful neuropathy is accompanied by orthostatic hypotension, gastrointestinal dysmotility, bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome and cardiomyopathy. What diagnosis merits focused assessment?', 'Amyloid neuropathy, including hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis',[
 ('Pure entrapment neuropathy as a complete explanation','Entrapments do not explain the generalized autonomic and cardiac syndrome.'),('Ordinary mild DSP with no systemic review','The multisystem red flags need investigation.'),('A negative family history excludes ATTRv','Penetrance, family recognition and de novo contexts can complicate history.')],
 'Look beyond the peripheral nerve', '**Autonomic dysfunction and cardiac disease are important amyloid clues.**', ['ATTRv and light-chain amyloidosis require different diagnostic and treatment pathways.','Carpal tunnel disease can precede broader manifestations.'], '**Confirm and type the disease appropriately.**', ['Assess monoclonal proteins and TTR genetics according to the pathway.','Specialist disease-directed therapy can alter management.'], [['Neuropathy','Sensory-motor/autonomic'],['Systemic clue','Cardiac or other organ involvement'],['Typing','Essential for treatment']], [AM], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Numbness affects the thumb, index and middle fingers at night with thenar weakness; the thenar eminence skin is relatively spared. Which localization best fits?', 'Median nerve compression at the carpal tunnel',[
 ('Ulnar nerve at the elbow','This more often affects the little finger and ulnar hand.'),('A complete C8 root lesion','The sensory territory and thenar pattern are more focal.'),('Radial nerve in the spiral groove','This mainly affects wrist/finger extension and a different sensory area.')],
 'A sensory branch can help localize the lesion', '**The palmar cutaneous median branch travels outside the carpal tunnel.**', ['Nocturnal symptoms and median-digit paresthesia are characteristic.','Severe or persistent motor deficit warrants prompt assessment.'], '**Use physiology when confirmation or severity assessment is needed.**', ['Consider splinting, local interventions or decompression according to severity.','Review systemic and occupational contributors.'], [['Median digits','Typical symptoms'],['Thenar eminence skin','Often spared'],['Thenar weakness','Motor involvement']], [PN], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Weak finger abduction and numbness in the little finger develop after prolonged elbow pressure. Which nerve is most likely affected?', 'Ulnar nerve',[
 ('Median nerve at the carpal tunnel','This primarily affects median digits and thenar muscles.'),('Posterior interosseous nerve','This is a motor branch affecting finger extension without this sensory pattern.'),('Long thoracic nerve','This causes serratus anterior weakness and scapular winging.')],
 'Interosseous weakness is an ulnar motor clue', '**The ulnar nerve supplies most intrinsic hand muscles and the ulnar sensory territory.**', ['Elbow compression is a common site.','Distinguish elbow disease from wrist lesions and C8/T1 pathology.'], '**Avoid further compression and assess severity.**', ['Persistent weakness or atrophy requires specialist assessment.','Examination and NCS can refine localization.'], [['Finger abduction','Interossei, ulnar nerve'],['Little finger','Ulnar sensory territory'],['Pressure site','Often elbow']], [PN], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Foot drop follows prolonged leg crossing. Dorsiflexion and eversion are weak but inversion is preserved. Which lesion is favored?', 'Common fibular nerve at the fibular head',[
 ('L5 radiculopathy as the more likely isolated pattern','L5 root disease can also weaken inversion through tibialis posterior.'),('Femoral nerve lesion','This primarily affects knee extension rather than this foot pattern.'),('Tibial nerve alone','This more often impairs plantar flexion and intrinsic foot function.')],
 'Test muscles outside the apparent nerve territory', '**Preserved inversion helps distinguish fibular neuropathy from a broader L5 lesion.**', ['Assess sensory findings and hip abduction as additional clues.','Nerve studies can confirm the site and injury type.'], '**Remove pressure and protect gait.**', ['An ankle-foot orthosis may reduce falls.','Persistent or progressive deficits need evaluation.'], [['Fibular nerve','Dorsiflexion/eversion'],['Tibialis posterior','Inversion via tibial nerve, L5'],['L5 root','Can involve both']], [PN], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Wrist and finger extension are weak after sleeping with the upper arm compressed; elbow extension is preserved. Which lesion is most likely?', 'Radial neuropathy near the spiral groove',[
 ('Median neuropathy at the wrist','This does not explain wrist drop.'),('Ulnar neuropathy at the elbow','This affects intrinsic hand and selected flexor muscles.'),('A complete high radial lesion proximal to triceps branches','That would be more likely to weaken elbow extension.')],
 'Preserved muscles help locate a nerve injury', '**A spiral-groove radial lesion commonly spares triceps while impairing distal extensors.**', ['Dorsal radial hand sensation may be affected.','A posterior interosseous lesion has a different motor-only pattern.'], '**Assess cause and functional protection.**', ['Use a suitable splint and avoid further compression.','Trauma or progressive findings require additional assessment.'], [['Spiral groove','Distal extensor weakness'],['Triceps','Often spared'],['Posterior interosseous','Motor-only distal pattern']], [PN], 'clinical_scenario')
q('How can sensory nerve action potentials help distinguish a typical radiculopathy from a distal peripheral nerve lesion?', 'They are often preserved in a preganglionic root lesion but can be reduced with postganglionic peripheral injury',[
 ('They are always absent in every radiculopathy','The dorsal-root ganglion is distal to many root lesions.'),('They measure only muscle membrane function','They record sensory nerve activity.'),('Normal sensory responses exclude all neuropathies','Small-fiber neuropathy and some motor neuropathies can have normal routine sensory responses.')],
 'The dorsal-root ganglion is the anatomical reference point', '**A lesion proximal to the ganglion can spare the distal sensory axon response.**', ['This is a useful tendency rather than an absolute rule.','Needle EMG and the clinical distribution add localization.'], '**Interpret all studies together.**', ['Plexus and nerve lesions are postganglionic.','Technical factors and coexisting neuropathy can complicate interpretation.'], [['Root proximal to DRG','Often preserved SNAP'],['Peripheral nerve','SNAP may be reduced'],['Needle EMG','Adds motor localization']], [EV,PN])
q('When is nerve biopsy most appropriately considered in a neuropathy work-up?', 'When a defined question such as vasculitis, amyloid or infiltration is likely to change management and less invasive assessment is insufficient',[
 ('Routinely before glucose testing in every DSP','Most typical DSP evaluations do not need biopsy.'),('Only to measure the amount of pain','Biopsy is not a generic pain-severity test.'),('Without discussing possible persistent sensory loss','Biopsy has morbidity and requires informed discussion.')],
 'An invasive test needs a treatment-relevant question', '**Biopsy can be valuable in selected patchy inflammatory or infiltrative disorders.**', ['Choose an appropriate affected nerve or combined tissue strategy.','Sampling error can occur.'], '**Balance yield against harm.**', ['Review noninvasive tests and safer tissue targets.','Discuss pain, wound complications and residual numbness.'], [['Typical DSP','Usually no biopsy'],['Vasculitis/infiltration','Selected diagnostic value'],['Decision','Yield and morbidity']], [VA,EV])

